<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fubing system</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fubing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fubing system was a militia system used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, in which farming households provided soldiers in return for land. It supplied troops cheaply, but as frontier wars demanded permanent professional armies it broke down in the eighth century and was replaced by standing forces under military governors.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The fubing militia was first employed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> under the administration of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, and was adopted in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang dynasties as the basis of their armies from the 6th to the 8th centuries. It supplemented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> system, which assigned every household a share of land to farm. The Sui placed the militia units under local civil administration, while the Tang brought them under metropolitan control through the Ministry of the Army. The name is written fǔbīng zhì in pinyin and fu-ping chih in Wade–Giles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Militiamen farmed their land when off duty and were mobilised again whenever a war occurred, and as reserves they could also be called up quickly in times of political instability; those living more than 2000 li away served for two months every 18 months. The units became hereditary military families and gave rise to large militarised settlements and communities. The Tang fielded 634 militia units, later called Zhechongfu, with every six families expected to provide one man. A unit had 800 to 1200 men, divided into tuan of 300, dui of 50 and huo of 10.</p><p>The units were concentrated in the north: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> alone fielded 261, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> 164, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> 74, together about 80 percent of fubing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>. Because the militiamen supported themselves by farming, the system cost the government little. It provided, however, only for peacetime and brief campaigns, since prolonged warfare kept the men from their fields, and it was gradually abandoned in favour of full-time army units known as jian'er.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fubing system” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>fubing</b> militia system, inherited from the Northern Zhou and Sui and refined by the early Tang, provided a force of farmer-soldiers whose service obligations were linked to land allocation, sparing the state the cost of a large permanent professional army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime named its offices after the classical Rites of Zhou to give itself ancient legitimacy, and relied on the <b>fubing</b> militia, which bound Xianbei and Han soldiers to the state through land grants.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a></li><li><blockquote>With the minister Su Chuo he issued administrative reforms, revived offices modelled on the Rites of Zhou and created the <b>fubing</b> militia, enrolling Chinese and Xianbei soldiers under Eight Pillars of State.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a></li></ul></section>
